1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,411
Permission denied.

2
00:00:01,431 --> 00:00:05,305
The most common error in Linux, and the message never tells you why.

3
00:00:05,325 --> 00:00:09,084
But there is a method to find out, and in this video you learn it.

4
00:00:09,554 --> 00:00:13,289
In this video: diagnose and correct file permission problems.

5
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:20,392
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:20,862 --> 00:00:23,615
Diagnose and correct file permission problems.

7
00:00:24,085 --> 00:00:25,592
We'll fix two real cases:

8
00:00:25,612 --> 00:00:28,952
a script that will not run, and a file nobody can read,

9
00:00:28,972 --> 00:00:31,212
where there is more than one problem.

10
00:00:39,732 --> 00:00:42,922
When access is denied, ask three questions. Who am I?

11
00:00:42,942 --> 00:00:45,830
Who owns the file, and what are its permissions?

12
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:51,403
And can I even reach the file, because every folder on the way needs the execute permission?

13
00:00:55,873 --> 00:00:58,371
Remember what the bits mean. On a file:

14
00:00:58,391 --> 00:01:02,181
read is read, write is change, execute is run. On a folder:

15
00:01:02,201 --> 00:01:06,250
execute means you may enter it, and read means you may list it.

16
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:10,125
Without execute on a folder, nothing inside it is reachable.

17
00:01:15,595 --> 00:01:20,622
Two commands do the diagnosis. L S dash L shows owner, group and permissions.

18
00:01:20,642 --> 00:01:22,850
Namei dash L walks the whole path,

19
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:26,521
and shows the permissions of every folder along the way.

20
00:01:26,541 --> 00:01:31,764
Before the demo, silently, the lab creates a script and a report file with wrong

21
00:01:31,784 --> 00:01:34,583
permissions. These steps change the system.

22
00:01:45,103 --> 00:01:48,797
First case. We run the script, and bash says: permission denied.

23
00:01:48,817 --> 00:01:51,931
The file was found, so the problem is its permissions.

24
00:01:56,401 --> 00:02:01,680
L S dash L shows the permissions: R W dash, R dash dash, R dash dash.

25
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:05,443
Nobody has the execute bit, so nobody can run it.

26
00:02:05,463 --> 00:02:09,821
The dot at the end is an S E Linux marker, a later topic.

27
00:02:16,291 --> 00:02:21,626
Chmod U plus X gives the owner the execute bit. Run it again, and it works.

28
00:02:27,146 --> 00:02:31,643
Second case. Cat says permission denied, and the file is four levels deep.

29
00:02:31,663 --> 00:02:33,291
Which level is the problem?

30
00:02:37,761 --> 00:02:42,496
Namei dash L walks the path one level at a time, and prints owner,

31
00:02:42,516 --> 00:02:47,900
group and permissions for each. Slash, S R V and proj are open to everyone.

32
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:52,943
But reports is D R W X followed by six dashes: only root can enter it.

33
00:02:52,963 --> 00:02:57,987
And namei says it on the last line: permission denied on q3 dot T X T.

34
00:03:07,507 --> 00:03:10,813
We fix the folder first. Chmod seven five five:

35
00:03:10,833 --> 00:03:16,192
the owner gets read, write and execute, everyone else gets read and execute.

36
00:03:16,212 --> 00:03:17,962
So everyone can enter it.

37
00:03:23,432 --> 00:03:27,392
But cat still says permission denied. There is a second problem.

38
00:03:27,412 --> 00:03:29,382
L S dash L on the file shows it:

39
00:03:29,402 --> 00:03:32,926
owner root, group root, and read and write for root only.

40
00:03:40,446 --> 00:03:45,617
Nobody but root has any access to this file. Chown student makes student the owner.

41
00:03:45,637 --> 00:03:47,743
Chown changes the owner of a file.

42
00:03:52,213 --> 00:03:54,397
Now cat works. Remember the method:

43
00:03:54,417 --> 00:03:59,120
read the error, look at the file with L S dash L, walk the path with namei,

44
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:02,647
and fix one thing at a time, checking after each change.

45
00:04:10,167 --> 00:04:13,603
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

46
00:04:15,123 --> 00:04:19,211
We reboot. Permissions are stored in the file system, so they stay.

47
00:04:19,231 --> 00:04:23,380
The script is still executable and runs, and the report still reads.

48
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:39,544
Your turn. The script run dot S H in your home folder will not run.

49
00:04:39,564 --> 00:04:45,044
The file plan dot T X T in slash S R V slash data slash private cannot be read.

50
00:04:45,064 --> 00:04:48,804
Find out why, with L S dash L and namei, and fix both.

51
00:04:48,824 --> 00:04:53,120
Do not make the folder writable for everyone. Run the checker.

52
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:02,341
Try before you open the solution.

53
00:05:07,861 --> 00:05:12,364
Next up: schedule tasks using at, cron, and systemd timer units.

54
00:05:12,834 --> 00:05:15,736
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
